\title{Circuits, Constraints, and Witness Generation}
\author{dhsorens}
\date{2026-08-24}

\p{
    A \code{Circuit F α} in Clean is a function from an offset to a value and a list of operations
    — \code{ℕ → α × List (Operation F)} — which is a writer monad accumulating operations
    mixed with a state monad threading the offset. The offset is the index of the next unallocated
    witness cell, and \code{bind} advances it by the number of cells the preceding operations
    allocated. That is the whole of the representation, and the \code{do} notation is what makes it
    readable: a circuit is written as a straight-line program that allocates cells and asserts
    equations, and the monad turns it into a list.
}

\p{
    There are five operations. \code{witness m ir} allocates #{m} cells and records how to compute
    them; \code{assert e} requires the expression #{e} to vanish; \code{lookup} requires a row to
    appear in a table; \code{interact} emits a channel interaction; \code{subcircuit} nests an
    already-proved circuit. Only three of the five are checked by the verifier. \code{witness}
    carries no constraint whatsoever — it is instruction to the prover, invisible in the
    constraint system.
}

\p{
    That asymmetry is where circuit bugs live. A circuit is two programs sharing one syntax: the
    constraint system the verifier checks, and the witness generator the prover runs. An
    under-constrained circuit is one whose generator computes the right value and whose constraints
    also permit a wrong one, and no amount of testing finds it, because testing runs the generator.
    Clean's response is to state soundness over the constraints alone, with the generator appearing
    only in the completeness statement — so the property that testing cannot reach is the property
    the soundness proof is about.
}

\p{
    Expressions are a small inductive type — variable, constant, addition, multiplication — so
    an \code{Expression F} is a polynomial in the witness cells, and \code{assertZero} is the only
    primitive constraint. Two pieces of notation cover most circuit code: \code{x === y} asserts an
    equality, and \code{let b <== e} allocates a cell and constrains it equal to #{e}, which is the
    idiom for naming an intermediate value that the verifier can refer to.
}

\p{
    Witness generation is written in a deep-embedded IR rather than as a Lean closure, and the
    reason is that the prover does not run in Lean. Because \code{WitgenIR} is data it can be
    serialised to a proving backend, checked for exportability, and still evaluated inside Lean by a
    reference interpreter, so one definition serves the proof and the Rust prover both. The
    authoring surface has two sorts: field-sorted expressions with #{+}, #{\times} and inverse, and
    a 64-bit integer sort with wrapping arithmetic and bitwise operations, plus conditionals,
    comparisons, and bit extraction. Gadgets work on bytes and 32-bit limbs, so the wraps are never
    taken, and the normalisation simproc erases them whenever the gadget's own assumptions bound the
    operand. Anything genuinely wider than 64 bits has to stay field-sorted.
}

\p{
    Two supporting classes make gadgets composable. \code{ProvableType} equips a type former with a
    \code{size}, maps to and from a flat vector of that many elements, and round-trip proofs —
    this is how a structured input becomes a run of witness cells, and record types get it by
    \code{deriving ProvableStruct}. \code{ElaboratedCircuit} carries the offset-independent facts
    about a circuit: how many cells it allocates and what it outputs, each with a proof that it
    agrees with actually running the circuit. Elaboration is what lets a caller use a gadget without
    unfolding its body, and it is inferred by tactic in the common case.
}
